{0 Nested projects}

An editor workspace may contain more than one OysterMark project. Projects are
ownership boundaries: each Markdown document belongs to exactly one project,
and vault operations do not implicitly cross from one project into another.

{1:roots Project roots}

The workspace root is always a project root, whether or not it contains an
[oysterlsp.json]. Any descendant directory containing an [oysterlsp.json] is
the root of a nested project.

For example:

{v
./oysterlsp.json
./a/oysterlsp.json
./a/note-in-a.md
./note-b.md
./note-c.md
v}

defines two projects. The outer project owns [note-b.md] and [note-c.md]. The
project rooted at [a] owns [a/note-in-a.md].

A document is owned by its {b nearest ancestor project root}. Equivalently,
project discovery starts at a root and prunes every subtree rooted at another
project. A directory without an [oysterlsp.json] is an ordinary directory and
remains in its nearest ancestor project.

This rule applies to all files used by the vault, not only Markdown notes. An
asset below [a] belongs to the project rooted at [a] and is absent from the
outer project's index.

{1:configuration Configuration}

Each project has one configuration. Its [oysterlsp.json], when present, is
read from the project root and overrides client [initializationOptions] field
by field as specified in {!page-"feature-configuration"}. Configuration is
project-scoped: it is neither inherited from the parent project nor merged
according to the edited document's directory.

The workspace-root project is the sole exception to requiring a marker: it
uses [initializationOptions] and defaults when no root [oysterlsp.json] exists.

{1:visibility Link visibility}

Links resolve only against the source document's owning project. Project
boundaries are symmetric:

{ul
  {- An outer note cannot resolve a note or asset owned by a nested project.}
  {- A nested note cannot resolve a note or asset owned by an outer project.}
  {- Sibling projects cannot resolve each other's notes or assets.}
}

This remains true when the authored target contains a relative path crossing
the boundary. If that target would exist only in another project, it is
unresolved in the source project. External links with a URI scheme retain
their ordinary non-vault meaning.

{1:imports Imports}

A project may selectively restore visibility into a descendant project by
listing its project-relative path:

{[
  {
    "imports": ["./a"]
  }
]}

An import makes the imported project's directly owned tree part of the
importer's vault {e view}. It is equivalent to not pruning that project root,
except that projects nested beneath the imported project remain pruned unless
they too are explicitly imported. Imports are therefore direct rather than
transitive.

Imported files keep their paths relative to the importing project. With the
example in {!section-roots}, [a/note-in-a.md] enters the outer index under that
path. Ordinary resolution is unchanged and runs over the combined view:

{ul
  {- [ [[a/note-in-a]] ] is an exact path match;}
  {- [ [[note-in-a]] ] may find it through the existing ordered-component
     subpath match.}
}

Imports do not introduce aliases, namespaces, or a second resolution pass.
Imported candidates participate in the same ranking as locally owned files.
This deliberately restores the resolution behavior the containing vault had
before the subtree became a project.

Import paths are resolved relative to the importing project root. A usable
path must normalize to a discovered descendant project root. Absolute paths,
paths containing [..], duplicate normalized paths, the project itself, and
paths that do not name a project are rejected and reported as configuration
warnings. Import changes take effect after restart.

Visibility is directional. If the outer project imports [a], an outer note
may resolve [a/note-in-a.md]. A note owned by [a] cannot import or resolve an
outer-project note: parent and sibling imports are not supported. Importing
changes visibility, not ownership or configuration.

{2:ancestor-imports Potential ancestor imports}

{b Not supported}: importing a parent project, for example ["imports":
[".."]]. The current implementation rejects every import containing [..],
even when normalization would land on a discovered ancestor project. This is
reported as a configuration warning rather than silently ignored.

If ancestor imports are supported later, they cannot be implemented merely by
stopping subtree pruning: a child project must instead merge a view of its
parent's directly owned files. Imported paths should remain relative to the
importer, and an exact local path should win over imported candidates.

For example:

{v
./oysterlsp.json
./a.md
./d.md
./b/oysterlsp.json
./b/c.md
./b/d.md
v}

If [b] imported its parent, the prospective importer-relative paths would be:

{ul
  {- [d.md] for [b/d.md];}
  {- [../d.md] for the outer [d.md];}
  {- [../a.md] for the outer [a.md].}
}

Under that potential behavior, [ [[d]] ] in [b/c.md] would resolve to the
local [b/d.md], while [ [[../d]] ] would resolve to the outer [d.md]. The two
files must not be flattened into one [d.md] namespace, since that would make
resolution depend on index order. Imported parent candidates would enter the
existing subpath ranking only when no exact local path matched.

This subsection records a possible extension, not accepted syntax or current
behavior.

{1:operations Vault operations}

Every vault-derived feature uses the source or target document's owning
project and its index. In particular:

{ul
  {- completion offers only members of that project;}
  {- go-to-definition and hover do not enter another project;}
  {- unresolved-link diagnostics treat a cross-boundary vault target like any
     other target absent from the project;}
  {- backlinks, find-references, reference counts, and graph statistics see
     only edges whose occurrences belong to that project;}
  {- rename edits references and files only within one project;}
  {- create-note and daily-note operations create files only within their
     project root.}
}

For an importing project, "that project" above means its combined vault view.
Creation and daily-note operations remain confined to the requesting project's
owned tree and never create through an import.

Reverse queries cross the visibility edge. References and backlinks to an
imported note include occurrences in every loaded importing project. Rename
updates the owner project and every loaded importer that can see the target;
duplicate edits arising because the same physical document occurs in several
views are applied once.

These constraints keep the graph internally consistent. In particular, a
reference cannot appear in one project's outgoing links while being invisible
to the destination project's backlinks or rename operation.

{1:routing Request routing}

The server maintains independent vault, index, configuration, and derived
state for each discovered project. An LSP request is routed by document URI to
the deepest project root that is an ancestor of that document. A URI below the
workspace root but outside every nested project therefore uses the
workspace-root project.

Open buffers belong to the same project as their filesystem path. Unsaved
contents may replace that project's indexed document, but do not change its
ownership or become visible to another project.

{1:lifecycle Lifecycle}

Project roots are discovered when the server initializes. Adding, removing,
or moving an [oysterlsp.json] changes ownership of a subtree and therefore
requires a server restart. Editing an existing configuration file likewise
takes effect after restart, as specified in
{!page-"feature-configuration".reload}.

Future file watching must update both affected projects atomically: no
request may observe the same document in both the old and new owner indexes.

{1:workspace-folders Workspace folders}

Nested projects describe ownership within one initialized workspace root.
They do not by themselves specify LSP [workspaceFolders] support. A future
multi-root server applies the same rules independently to each workspace
folder and must choose the deepest matching project root when routing a URI.

{1:implementation Implementation}

{!Lsp_lib.Server.initialize} discovers project markers and constructs one
pruned vault state per project. Document handlers route through the project
boundary in {!module-Lsp_lib.Server}. Focused ownership, configuration,
definition, and reference tests are in
{!module-Test_lsp.Test_nested_projects}.
